  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nThere is not cloud in the sky according to GOES visible\nsatellite imagery. Temperatures have increased accordingly,\ndespite a cold front passing through the area, with upper 60s\nand low 70s currently. High temperatures should end up in the\nmid 70s with isolated spots south of I-80 with a chance to touch\nthe upper 70s. These clear skies will remain through the\novernight allowing for better radiational cooling and dropping\ntemperatures down into the low 40s with typical colder spots\npossibly falling into the upper 30s.\n\nWhile surface high pressure is expected over Chicagoland, broad\nnorthwest flow will remain in place through the coming week. \nSeasonable temperatures can be expected through much of the week \nwith a slight warmer bump in temps mid week as winds turn\nsouthwest for a moment. A few weak perturbations may traverse \nthe northwest flow late Tuesday into Wednesday and again on \nThursday. Not only have models consistently been showing the \ncore of these systems remaining over Wisconsin, but they appear \nfairly moisture starved. While a sprinkle cannot be completely\nruled out, PoPs remain below any formal mention in the forecast.\nThis dry and seasonable trend looks to remain in place through\nnext weekend.\n\nDK",
